titre$ Key to the European species of the genus Hohenbuehelia
com$ Adapt GF 30 nov 2018
1,0$source : "The Genera Hohenbuehelia and Resupinatus in Europe" ///Giovanni Consiglio(Author), Ledo Setti(Author)/// 2017 Monografie di Pagine di Micologia Volume: 3
1,0$


1,2 $ Species sessile, with a dimidiate, orbicular or cup-shaped pileus
1,13 $ Species with a more or less differentiated stipe
2,3 $ Pileus dimidiate-orbicular, reaching 6 cm, covered with a white felt consisting of concentric tufts and spinulose-gelatinous, more or less erect and fasciculate scales; spores oblong
2,4 $ Pileus smaller or without felt consisting of spinulose-gelatinous scales
3,0 $ Pileus grey, grey-brown to blue-grey; spores on average 7.5 x 4.7 µm (Vm = 90 µm3 ), pileipellis without gloeosphexes, on logs, stems and fallen branches of hardwood $ Hohenbuehelia mastrucata
3,0,n $H. mastrucata, a species of broad-leaved trees, is easily recognizable for the blue-grey or grey-brown pileus, decorated with a glossy white felt as concentric tufts and spinulose-gelatinous scales

3,0 $ Pileus brownish grey to reddish brown; spores on average 7.7 x 5.0 µm(Vm = 101 pileipellis with gloeosphexes, on decaying fallen branches or bark $ Hohenbuehelia valesiaca
3,0,n $It differs from H. mastrucata for the larger spores, the much greater average thickness of pilei trama, the presence of gloeosphexes and the habitat on softwood.

4,5 $ Pileus > 2 cm broad, dimidiate, kidney-, shell- to fan-shaped, whitish, pale grey-brown, honey brown, dark brown, olive brown, with a whitish to concolorous tomentum towards the base; spores oblong
4,6 $ Pileus < 2 cm broad
5,0 $ Lamellae more or less distant, white to pale yellowish, spores on average 7.3 x 3.9 µm (Vm = 59 µm3), pileipellis without gloeosphexes, on decaying fallen branches of hardwood, rarely on softwood $ Hohenbuehelia grísea
5,0,n $They can be merged into a single taxon variable by pileus and lamellae colours, all of the chromatic variants of H. grísea that, however, display the same morphochromatic paradigm, that is, always without bluish shades, lamellae more or less distant, white to pale yellowish, spores ellipsoid to cylindrical, basidia 4-spored, hairs emerging from epicutis clustered in tufts, pileocystidia absent.

5,0 $ Lamellae rather crowded, whitish or cream then yellow to yellow-orange, spores on average 72,0 x 4.1 µm(V = 65 µm3), pileipellis with gloeosphexes, on decaying fallen branches of hardwood, rarely on softwood $ Hohenbuehelia atrocoerulea
5,0,n $It should be noted that it is sometimes very difficult to separate the H. atrocoerulea collections with a pileus without bluish tones from those of H. grísea. In such cases, the search for pileogloeosphexes becomes fundamental.

6,7 $ Lamellae at first white or whitish
6,10 $ Lamellae at first dark
7,8 $ Pileus cup-shaped, lamellae little numerous and distant, spores allantoid-cylindrical
7,9 $ Pileus dimidiate, lamellae moderately crowded, spores oblong
8,0 $ Pileus cyphelloid or cup-shaped, pale grey to grey-brown, in dried specimen^ blackish under an ash-grey scurf, lamellae white to pale creamy ochre, spores on average 8.2 x 3.6 µm (Vm = 59 µm3 ), on decaying fallen branches or bark of hardwood or on decaying herbaceous stems, rarely on conifers $ Hohenbuehelia cyphelliformis
8,0,n $H. cyphelliformis is well characterized by its non-blackening white lamellae, the pileus covered with minute white flakes, the spores cylindrical to allantoid, the cheilometuloids with little thick, but yet visible in cresyl blue, walls, the epicutis with hyphae encrusted with a coarse brownish pigment, the presence of pileogloeosphexes and the habitat on herbaceous plants.

8,0 $ Pileus cup- or shell-shaped, black or blackish brown in young specimens, then grey- brown, lamellae white to ash-grey, drying creamy-yellowish, spores on average 10.1 x 4.8 µm (Vm = 126 µm3), on decaying fallen branchlets or on decaying herbaceous stems $ Hohenbuehelia leightonii
8,0,n $H. leightonii is recognized for the pileus surface minutely hispid but glabrescent towards the margin, hispid to hairy-tomentose towards the point of attachment, the spores much larger and the habitat on decaying fallen branchlets or on decaying herbaceous stems.

9,0 $ Pileus fan- or shell-shaped, pale grey-brown, at first completely glutinous-viscid, spores cylindrical, on the average 9.0 x 4.4 µm (V = 93 µm3), basidia 2-spored, on dead branches of hardwood $ Hohenbuehelia fluxilis
9,0,n $At best, H. fluxilis could be defined as a H. grisea with 2-spored instead of 4-spored basidia but in H.fluxilis basidia and spores are significantly larger than those of H. grisea.

9,0 $ Pileus dimidiate, kidney-, shell- or fan-shaped, blue-grey covered with a concolorous tomentum towards the base, lamellae rather crowded, whitish or cream then yellow to yellow-orange, spores oblong, on average 7.2 x 4.1 (V = 65 µm3 ), basidia generally 4-spored, on decaying fallen branches of hardwood, rarely on softwood $ Hohenbuehelia atrocoerulea
9,0,n $It should be stressed that it is sometimes very difficult to separate the H. atrocoerulea collections with a pileus without bluish tones from those of H. grisea. In such cases, the search for pileogloeosphexes becomes fundamental.

10,11 $ Metuloids immersed and hardly visible, pileus cup-shaped, lamellae silvery grey, on drying grey-brown to black
10,12 $ Metuloids emerging, very obvious, pileus cup-shaped to dimidiate
11,0 $ On branches of hardwood or on shrubs, pileus grey-brown to blackish with silvery grey tomentum, spores oblong, on average 7.1 x 4,0  µm  (V =61 µm3) $ Hohenbuehelia unguicularis
11,0,n $H. unguicularis is well characterized by the pileus covered with a dense fluff, the lamellae silvery grey, blackening, the hairy pseudostipe, the spores ellipsoid to cylindrical, the cheilometuloids clavate, brown and thick-walled towards the base and thin-walled towards the apex, sometimes hard to detect but clearly visible in cresyl blue, the trichodermoid hairs emerging from the epicutis grouped in tufts

11,0 $ On branches of conifers, pileus black, glossy, covered with fine tufts scattered of white fibrils, spores cylindrical, on average 7.5 x 3.6 µm (V =51 µm3 ) $ Hohenbuehelia josserandii
11,0,n $H. pinacearum is distinguished for its exclusive habitat on conifers, the spores oblong, the pileus covering almost glabrous, at most covered with a delicate whitish grey scurf, the cheilometuloids immersed, hardly visible, thick-walled downwards, thin-walled towards the apex, easily collapsed, and the pileus covering provided with gloeosphexes.

12,0 $ On wood of conifers, pileus pale ash grey, then dark grey to black, covered with a whitish to brownish tomentum towards the base, lamellae soon grey-brown to black, spores oblong, on average 8.3 x 4.6 µm (Vm = 94 µm 3) $ Hohenbuehelia mustialensis
12,0,n $H. mustialensis resembles H. leightonii, but differs microscopically for the spores, mostly oblong, significantly shorter, for the shorter and narrower basidia and for the habitat on softwood.

12,0 $ On hardwood, pileus dark grey-blue, covered with a less coarse tomentum, lamellae dark grey-blue to black, spores oblong, on average 6.8 x 4.2 µm (V = 63 µm3 ) $ Resupinatus niger
12,0,n $R. niger can be distinguished from the three very small-sized species of Hohenbuehelia with pileus and lamellae dark to blackish grey, that is, H. cyphelliformis, H. pinacearum and H. unguicularis, for the presence of prominent metuloids, visible on the faces even by a lens with a magnification of * ten. Unlike the three above-mentioned species, R. niger can have a pileus with violaceous or even blue colours, covered with an important felt, especially towards the point of attachment to the substrate. From a microscopic point of view, it is strongly characterized by the presence of gel everywhere in the basidiome. The cross section of lamellae displays a very special, "spoon-like", outline, peculiar to this species.

13,14 $ Species with a more or less central stipe
13,19 $ Species without a central stipe, with a pileus "petaloid" to fan-shaped, semifunnel-shaped or spathulate to spoon-shaped
14,0 $ Pileus funnel-shaped, sooty to greyish, stipe up to 6 cm long, spores subglobose (Qm 1,13), on a dead log of Fraxinus angustifolia, so far only found in France $ Hohenbuehelia boullardii
14,0,n $H. boullardii differs from all the other Hohenbuehelia species provided with a central stipe, mostly for the subglobose spores.

14,15 $ Spores oblong (Qm 1,60-3,08)
15,16 $ Stipe up to 2 cm long
15,17 $ Stipe up to 8 cm long
16,0 $ Pileus blackish grey to reddish brown, spores oblong, on average 7.9 x 4.5  µm  (V = 86 µm3 ), on sandy soil, at the base of Poaceae $ Hohenbuehelia ilerdensis
16,0,n $Among the Hohenbuehelia species provided with a differentiated stipe and with an omphaloid to clitocyboid habit, H. ilerdensis is characterized by the small size, the pileus colour and the ecology graminicolous on arid soils.

16,0 $ Pileus brown-black to black, spores ellipsoid, on average 9.3 x 5.6  µm  (Vm = 163 µm3), on sandy soils, on culms of Ammophila arenaria $ Hohenbuehelia culmicola
16,0,n $H. culmicola is easily recognized for its well-developed stipe and for the growth in sandy dunes on herb, mainly of Ammophila arenaria, stems.

17,0 $ Pileus avana brown, honey brown, stipe up to 8 cm long, oblong spores, on average 9.0 =< 5.1  µm  (Vm = 128 µm3), in marshy places among Aulacomnium or near shrubs of Polytrichum, Betula nana or Salix $ Hohenbuehelia longipes
17,0,n $H. longipes is characterized by the growth in marshy places or in peatlands and, from a micromorphological point of view, for the relatively little gelatinous cutis and the presence of pileogloeosphexes.

17,0 $ Pileus black-brown, stipe up to 6 cm long, spores cylindrical, on average 11.4 x 4.1  µm  (Vm =102 µm3), gregarious and in tufts under Quercus ilex or pines $ Hohenbuehelia faerberioides
17,0,n $H. faerberioides is well characterized by its bulky and fleshy habit, the central stipe, the tobacco brown pileus that contrasts with the white lamellae and, microscopically, by the large oblong spores and the relatively little gelatinous pileipellis.

19,20 $ Species with a spathulate to spoon-shaped pileus
19,21 $ Species with a "petaloid" or semi-funnel-shaped pileus
20,0 $ Pileus spathulate, at first dirty white then pale brown. Lamellae crowded and narrow. Spores very small, subglobose to broadly ellipsoid (Qm 1.22), on average 4.8 x 3.9  µm  (Vm = 39 µm3), pileipellis provided with pileogloeosphexes and pileometuloids, in woods of broad-leaved trees $ Hohenbuehelia wilhelmii
20,0,n $The salient characteristics of this species are the spathulate to spoon-shaped pileus, covered with a fine white down; the crowded and narrow lamellae, the presence of pileogloeosphexes and pileometuloids.

20,0 $ Pileus dirty white, pale grey-brown, yellowish brown to dark brown. Lamellae crowded, deeply decurrent, white. Spores larger and differently shaped (Qm 1,42), on average 5.7 x 4.0  µm  (Vm = 50 µm3 ), pileipellis with pileogloeosphexes and pileometuloids, on fallen branches and logs or on decaying stumps of hardwood and softwood $ Hohenbuehelia auriscalpium
20,0,n $It is recognized by its pileus covered with a fine white down; the crowded and deeply decurrent lamellae; the relatively small spores, the hairs emerging from epicutis clustered in tufts, the presence of pileogloeosphexes and pileometuloids. H. wilhelmii has smaller and differently shaped spores with respect to H. auriscalpium, shorter basidia and cuticle soon glabrous. Molecular analysis clearly separates the two species, which occupy distant positions in the phylogenetic tree (see Chapter on "Molecular analysis").********

21,0 $ a Spores ellipsoid, on average 6.4 x 4.0 µm (V = 55 µm3); on the ground, on buried woody residues, or on fallen decaying branches or bark; in woods of broadleaved trees or under conifers, pileipellis with pileogloeosphexes and pileometuloids $ Hohenbueheliapetaloides
21,0,n $It is distinguished by the relatively small spores, the very thick gelatinous layer and pileitrama and the pileipellis made up of erect hyphae, clustered in tufts. There are also generally pileometuloids and, more rarely, pileogloeosphexes. The microscopic structure of the pileus covering results in a macroscopic appearance of cuticle, opaque, glabrous to finely pruinose near the margin, densely and finely floccose-scurfy, felted, velvety, tomentose towards the stipe.

21,0 $ b Spores oblong, on average 7.2 x 4.5  µm  (Vm = 76  µm 3), in forests with evergreen oaks and pines, on the ground or on buried woody debris, so far only found in Spain $ Hohenbuehelia pseudopetaloides
21,0,n $It differs from H. petaloides mainly for the slightly more elongated spores and the habitat in Mediterranean thermophilous forests .

21,0 $ c Spores ellipsoid, on average 7.0 x 4.6  µm  (Vm = 77 ( µm3 ), in woods of broadleaved trees or under conifers, on the ground, on buried woody residues, or on fallen decaying branches or bark $ Hohenbuehelia thornii
21,0,n $The less thick gelatinous layer, about a half that of H. petaloides, causes the epicutis hyphae to be less vertically erected to form tufts of trichodermoid hyphae. The microscopic structure of the pileipellis results in a macroscopic appearance of the cuticle that is more pruinose than felted, velvety or tomentose. Pileometuloids and, more rarely, pileogloeophexes are generally present.

21,0 $ d Spores ellipsoid, on average 7.2 x 4.7 µm (V = 86 µm 3), on the ground or on needles litter in coniferous woods $ Hohenbuehelia tremula
21,0,n $It is distinguished by the relatively large spores, the modest thickness of the pileitrama and the habitat in coniferous forests. In addition, pileogloeophexes and, more rarely, pileometuloids are generally present

21,0 $ e Spore ellipsoid, on average 8.1 x 5.6 µm (Vm = 134 | µm3 ), habitat on culms of coastal plants, mostly Ammophila arenaria $ Hohenbuehelia bonii
21,0,n $It is recognized by the spore dimensions (the largest of the complex) and by the marine habitat on culms of coastal plants.

